[00:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'This little square has been sitting on my desk for a few years. It is an air net 4 and is considered a very high quality home CO2 monitor. It\'s currently $189 US and it has just one job. It tells me the CO2 level in this room. For those that don\'t know, once your CO2'

[00:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'level gets around 1400 parts per million or higher, your ability to think clearly measurably drops. So when Unify announced a $99 PoE powered sensor that measures not just CO2, but temp, humidity, particulates, VOC, and even'

[00:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'has vape detection, all managed in Unify Protect, I had one question. Is the CO2 reading actually accurate? If that is all you wanted to know, that answer is yes. And those of you want to know more, well, keep watching. And full disclosure'

[00:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'up front, Unify did send me this device for testing, so I\'ve had it for a few months, but all opinions are my own. Let\'s dive deeper into this. The one thing I want to mention right up front is a prerequisite for this device is Unify Protect. Whether it\'s one of the'

[00:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Unify Protect NVRs or one of their gateways that has Unify Protect built in, that is necessary for this to connect. It\'s not a standalone product on its own for $99. It does have to connect to the Unify Protect ecosystem'

[01:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'in order to get any information or data out of it. And being that it\'s PoE, I receive PoE as a requirement. It does not ship with a PoE injector in the box, but you could power it that way. I\'m just powering it off one of my Unify PoE'

[01:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'switches, but obviously any PoE switch should work perfectly fine. Of note as well, it does have, in the box, a complete mounting kit. I just have it sitting on my desk. Works fine that way, but it does come with a little mounting'

[01:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'plate if you wanted to bolt it to a wall, to a ceiling, wherever you feel it might need to be mounted. And they left little gaps here, so the cable can run behind it, and it\'s a pretty straightforward setup for mounting. And it has this little screwdriver that sets'

[01:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'a set screw to lock it in place, so it can\'t easily just be spun and removed from the wall. Unify air quality adopts, like any other Unify Protect device, you simply put it on the same network as your Unify Protect, and it\'s adopted and'

[01:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'it automatically handles the firmware update. When you click on the device, it\'ll give you all the readings, but of course, you can go here, and we\'ll get to this in a moment, the sensor manager, where you can track it over time. In terms of setting it up in statistics,'

[02:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'it does have individual data for each of these, and I do have two of them. This is the one in my living room, and this is the one in my studio. The one in the studio is showing a lot more CO2 because I\'m in here recording right now. As the'

[02:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'HVAC system cycles the air in this room, it will pull it back down, and all the settings are adjustable. You can give the system a name. You can pair it with a camera, that way you can have the two in the same room and tie the information and correlate it together. I don\'t have'

[02:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'any cameras in my studio that are UniFi Protect. You can turn the status light on and off, the dashboard widget. You can also choose the LED status mode. You can say measure CO2 and let me know when it\'s'

[02:47] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'red for unhealthy, orange for poor, yellow for moderate, or green for good, or change to air quality, and the same, apply, but we have a couple extra colors based on the range, and it has a night mode and a brightness adjustment. I\'m'

[02:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'going to leave it on CO2. Coming down here, the events to capture, by default, all of these are checked, but you can add safe zones for notifications. Values outside the safe zone will trigger alerts. What this allows you to do is say whenever it gets'

[03:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'above, maybe 1,900 parts per million for CO2, go ahead and create an alert. You create these alerts over in the alarm manager. And this goes for any of the other events that are in here, whether it\'s humidity, temperature, VOC, TVOC,'

[03:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'or particulates. You have the options for any of these. You control what you decide is or is not a safe zone, whether it\'s too low or too high, and then you can say alert me outside of these zones.'

[03:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And creating alert is really simple. You go to create a new alarm, you choose a sensor, you say extreme values, above safe zone, below safe zone, or both, and it also has a separate vape detected. Then you choose what sensors you want to'

[03:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'apply to it. Then you have notify webhook, unlock, sound, etc. for the actions and the actions can be stacked. You can also have it send emails. So, the system gives you a lot of flexibility in terms of notifying you whenever there\'s an issue. Coming over'

[04:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'here to the sensor manager, you can track this over time. This is a 1-month range looking at the CO2, but I could also switch it to look at the humidity over time or even the VOC, vape, etc., all the different options. We come over'

[04:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'here to CO2 and we switch it here to July 4th, for example. Put July 4th there. Have it select July 4th here. Hit apply.'

[04:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And you can see the CO2 levels being a little bit higher because we had the doors closed. It was a very, very hot 4th of July here. And then when I invite my friends over, you see it spiking quite a bit more. Gets all the way up to the 15, 1600 from people being in the'

[04:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'house. And this is one of those reasons I like to monitor this. So, let\'s we know if we need to recirculate some of the air from the outside and if we\'re getting into really high or even dangerous CO2 levels. Now, one thing I\'ll note about the vaping, I don\'t'

[04:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'vape, but I do have some friends that do. So, I invited my friends over to vape. And they certainly were able to set off the sensors. And they set off the sensor here. The thing that\'s interesting is this is not my friend\'s'

[05:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'vaping that set off the sensor. This is burning a pizza in the oven setting off the sensor. I thought it was interesting that it picks up the smell of burnt cheese as vape at a very high vape confidence, but I imagine there\'s'

[05:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'probably other things you can burn that may also cause that type of false positive. But, that is the only false positive I had is one burnt pizza. If we scroll down here on the side, these are above or below thresholds you can set to filter for only specific sensors that'

[05:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'are showing those numbers. I only have two sensors, but if you had a whole lot of them, this may be a handy way to find which ones aren\'t at the thresholds you expect them to be at for a certain date range. We can also go down here and clear filters and then we can click on'

[05:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'sensor settings. Sensor settings let you group set all your sensors at once. Maybe you want to add a safe zone for all of them for a certain CO2, vape, etc. And then apply those changes. I like that it\'s got a group option, so'

[05:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you don\'t have to go individually into a lot of sensors. I can imagine this be a big deal in a large building maybe that has numerous sensors scattered around you want to set predefined thresholds for all of those. Now, I haven\'t done any testing with this, but I know people'

[06:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'may be wondering, can you pull the sensor data out into an API to maybe pull it into Home Assistant or charted however you\'d like? Yes, I looked at the API and the temperature settings, humidity, etc. are all listed right here. I have not tested it, but the fact'

[06:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that it got a documented API means this might be a fun little project for the future. Now, I mentioned accuracy at the beginning compared to the Airnet and they both are within, I would say, 5% of each other. There\'s also a slightly'

[06:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'tighter tolerance on the Airnet of plus or minus 3% across its range versus Ubiquiti is plus or minus 3% on the lower range and plus or minus 5% on the higher range of CO2. But, that\'s just'

[06:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'CO2. Overall, I think that\'s plenty close enough to know if you have too much CO2 and it\'s probably time to open a window. But, uh this does measure a lot faster because this runs on batteries and well, the batteries will be sacrificed if you'

[06:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'told you update constantly and frequently. Matter of fact, as I stand here and hold this and probably just breathe on it, I\'m pretty sure it\'s going to turn yellow. I don\'t know, we\'ll watch for that. Oh, there it goes. Completely on cue here. As always, leave your thoughts and comments down below or'

